---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { projects } from '../../data/showcase';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
---
<BaseLayout title="Projects" description="A selectively published portfolio of tangible projects, including the decisions, engineering, and evidence behind each one.">
  <header class="page-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">SELECTED WORK</p>
    <h1>Projects</h1>
    <p class="lead">I enjoy following an idea far enough that it becomes something I can build, test, change, and understand more deeply.</p>
  </header>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="making-work">
    <div class="body-copy narrative-copy">
      <h2 id="making-work">Building is part of how I learn.</h2>
      <p>My interests often begin with a question about what a system could do. Sometimes that leads to a home-lab experiment or a software prototype. Other times it becomes an educational tool, a model, a technical reference, or a way of exploring an unfamiliar platform. The common thread is that making something forces an idea to become specific enough to test.</p>
      <p>I don't consider every experiment a completed project, and I don't want this space to be an automatically generated inventory of repositories. For a project to appear here, there should be something tangible to examine and a story worth telling about how it came together.</p>
      <p>When I share a project, I want visitors to understand more than its name and technology stack. I want to explain the problem, the constraints, the choices I made, what I was able to demonstrate, and what I learned from the outcome. A project can still be evolving, but the specific result I present should exist and be inspectable.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="selected-projects-heading">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">EVIDENCE BEFORE PRESENTATION</p><h2 id="selected-projects-heading">Work selected for public exploration.</h2></div>
      <p>Each featured entry includes a demonstrable result and a path into the technical decisions behind it.</p>
    </div>
    {projects.length > 0 ? (
      <div class="card-grid">
        {projects.map(project => (
          <article class="card">
            <p class="status">{project.status === 'completed' ? 'Completed' : 'Demonstrable'} · {project.category}</p>
            <h3><a href={base + '/projects/' + project.slug + '/'}>{project.title}</a></h3>
            <p>{project.summary}</p>
            <a class="text-link" href={base + '/projects/' + project.slug + '/'}>Explore the project story ↗</a>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    ) : (
      <div class="curation-empty" role="status">
        <h3>I'm choosing what belongs here.</h3>
        <p>This gallery is intentionally selective. I haven't published a project case study in it yet. In the meantime, my <a href={base + '/knowledge/'}>Security Knowledge library</a> is a working public system that can be explored directly, and my <a href={base + '/about/interests/'}>personal interests</a> explain the kinds of things I'm drawn to building.</p>
      </div>
    )}
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="narrative-panel">
      <h2>What I want a project page to answer</h2>
      <p>A good project story should leave someone with a clear picture of what was built, why those choices made sense, and what evidence supports the result. I want the architecture, experiments, tradeoffs, and limitations to be just as discoverable as the finished interface.</p>
      <p>That's why I use progressive case studies: a short overview for somebody browsing, followed by deeper sections for somebody interested in the engineering.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href={base + '/about/capabilities/'}>Read about my technical practices ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
